module gunbc.plans.v2_compiler_architecture

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanProjectsCommittedMarkdown }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock, MarkdownTableRow, CodeBlock, TableBlock, AlignNone }
import gunbc.plans.md_helpers { h2, h3, p, li, ul, ol, cell, row, quote }

fn code(text: String) -> MarkdownBlock { CodeBlock { code: text } }

fn table3(header: MarkdownTableRow, rows: List<MarkdownTableRow>) -> MarkdownBlock {
  TableBlock { header: header, alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone], rows: rows }
}

fn r3(a: String, b: String, c: String) -> MarkdownTableRow { row(cells: [cell(text: a), cell(text: b), cell(text: c)]) }

fn v2_compiler_architecture_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    quote(blocks: [
      p(text: "**Status: the top-down design authority for the v2 compiler, one level below DESIGN.md; established 2026-10-01 (operator direction, session wonderful-feynman, on the second review of #12872 finding that the compiler's defining law was housed inside a program-runtime provisioning design). No implementation lands from this document.** It owns the universal law its children specialize, the pipeline those children sit in, the role of lenses, and the plan document graph that joins them. DESIGN.md stays the axioms and their consequences; this document is where 'what it means to author a v2 program' is stated once. The children it governs are listed under the derived heading above; the relation is authored once in `gunbc.plan_governance` and nowhere else."),
      p(text: "**Operator intention carried (the operator's words, 2026-09-30 to 2026-10-01).** 'If you forget to model this, the interface will not let you use it, at compile time.' 'The developer tells us what we do NOT know, versus the developer specifying the entire world repeatedly.' 'For materialization I want to tell the developer when we cannot cache something, or do not know how -- this is the opposite of normal programming.' 'With CLI args, the flags you pass are actually decisions or policy that can be modeled or derived from other inputs; only ask the developer for what is truly irreducible.' 'If we want to coerce Rust to Python, the compiler should do everything within its power and then stop when it is unaware or needs a decision from the user.' 'I want as much to be derived from the structure as physically possible.'"),
      p(text: "**Review of 14f36a479f folded in (2026-10-01, request changes; every finding accepted).** The pipeline placed selection after the closure that depends on it; section 3 now fixes static selection and binding BEFORE completion, as inputs that decide which holes exist, and names what follows the seal as the realization of choices already made. The over-asking ratchet is two measurements: AvoidableOverAsk under a fixed contract is monotone downward, DiscoveredResidual is not and is attributed as a fidelity discovery. The plan-governance lookup silently took the first row for a child with two parents; the relation is now an ADMITTED FUNCTIONAL GRAPH (sole_constructor, minted only by admission after functional, rooted, acyclic and complete are proven, with one typed refusal per defect) and every projection and lens consumes only that graph -- no first-match operation survives admission; the witness carries the two-parents, same-parent-twice, root-with-governor, hidden-cycle-in-both-orders, cycle, orphan and ordinary-chain controls. The pipeline is the static-completion fixed point with the decision/execution split stated noun by noun. The environmental specialization's provenance rung and its fsmonitor residual are corrected there."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "1. The law: compiler completion"),
    p(text: "v2 accepts a partial authored program and computes its maximal unique lawful completion from the facts already modeled -- the types and their sole constructors, the selected realizations and their upstream facts, the canonical catalogs, the composition root's policies, the registered providers and the bound observers. It never asks an author to restate a fact that structure, a canonical authority, selected policy or a bound observer can provide. What it cannot derive becomes a typed, located RESIDUAL carrying its provenance and the party that can answer it. Libraries may expose residuals; runnable programs and deployments must CLOSE them. Supplying an already-derivable fact is a second authority unless explicitly expressed as a policy departure with its reason (DESIGN section 3b)."),
    p(text: "MAXIMAL does not mean guessing, and it does not mean trying alternatives until one works:"),
    code(text: "one unique lawful route        -> complete silently\nno lawful route                -> residual\nmultiple lawful routes         -> residual\nthe route changes semantics    -> residual\nthe fact belongs to the world  -> residual addressed to an observer\nthe fact is a genuine choice   -> residual addressed to the root (policy)\nthe fact is the feature's own input -> residual addressed to the caller\nthe fact was never modeled     -> residual addressed to the upstream module\n"),
    p(text: "The review question that follows from the law, asked of every interface: why is the developer supplying this -- is it actually residual? Its two measured defects: OVER-ASKING, a residual addressed to a call site that structure or a root could have answered, is a counted modeling defect with a location, and the measurement is two, not one: holding the authored contract, route universe, root policy and observer surface FIXED, avoidable call-site over-asking (AvoidableOverAsk: a call-site question whose answer is already derivable under that fixed contract) may only shrink; the total residual population is NOT monotone, because modeling newly discovered behavior -- a hidden child spawn, a missing environmental requirement, a second lawful candidate, a subject that turns out to vary by placement or configuration -- exposes new residuals (DiscoveredResidual), and those are fidelity discoveries attributed as such, never regressions and never a reason to reward the compiler for staying ignorant; UNDER-ASKING, a completion that fills a hole from ambient state -- PATH, a framework default device, 'probably apt', a best-effort cast -- is fail-open and is refused by construction, because no route reads ambient state. Silence means fully derived; a residual means genuinely unknown; nothing exists between them."),

    h3(text: "1.1 Completion is a partition of routes by type, never a search"),
    p(text: "An omitted operand is a typed hole, and the hole's TYPE admits exactly one completion route: a placement is projected from the effect's host binding; an environmental value (`MaterializedProgram`, `PresentPrincipal`, `OwnedDirectory`) is completed by the provider the root registers for its target; a package coordinate is a catalog lookup; a materialized artifact is a `std.materialization_ladder` judgment; a grant is an admission; a lease is a lease; an observed fact is its observer; a representation crossing is a `v2.std.coercion` homomorphism; a plain `Int` is never completed. The routes are a partition, not a preference order: completion never tries one route and falls back to another, because a first-route-that-works search is the absorbing fallback of DESIGN section 5 moved into elaboration. A hole with zero routes or two routes is a residual, not a guess. When a route's input is not yet in scope -- the placement is unknown inside a library function -- the hole propagates outward as an inferred requirement of the enclosing function, which is how a library stays open and an executable closes."),
    p(text: "Resolution is subject-indexed (git at srv5 for deploy-runner, not 'a program'), static, provenance-carrying, sealed with the graph before the demand engine runs, and refuses zero or multiple answers. That is the distinction from dependency injection, which resolves by type at runtime and silently picks, and from a transpiler, which emits the closest construct and says nothing."),
    h3(text: "1.2 A residual is a question with an addressee"),
    p(text: "A residual is not an error. It is a typed statement of what the compiler does not know and WHO can answer it, and the addressee is part of the type: the call site (an irreducible input: a repository path, a requested model, the operation's own subject), the root (a policy choice: apt versus immutable image, observe-only versus may-apply, a tolerated staleness, Result as a class or as exceptions), an upstream module (a fact never modeled: which git operations spawn children under which configuration keys, a target language row that does not exist), or an observer (which probe reads the running image digest, which toolchain version is present). Each residual carries the provenance path through which the hole was reached, and that same path is the diagnostic when closure fails. A runtime refusal or a contended outcome is not a residual: those are typed outcomes of a closed program, owned by DESIGN section 5."),
    h3(text: "1.3 Closure: libraries may be open, runnable roots may not"),
    p(text: "A library function's interface is its irreducible parameters plus its inferred requirements. Binding a program to a root -- an executable, a deployment, a placement -- is what forces every requirement to a route, and the root is admitted only when the residual set addressed to anyone other than its own call sites is empty. A hole whose subject is only known at runtime cannot be completed at plan time and is a residual addressed to the call site: make the subject static or supply an observer. It is never completed by a runtime fallback."),
    h3(text: "1.4 Completion and coercion are one procedure"),
    p(text: "DESIGN section 4 already rules that emission, ingestion and coercion are one decision procedure run in different directions, and that an implicit coercion route must be total, exact, semantics-preserving, unique, pure and consent-free or refuse. Completion of a partial program is that procedure run forward over holes; coercion is it run sideways across two models; emission is it run backward over the target's rows. The outcomes are the same three in every direction -- an image exists uniquely (silent), an image exists under a proof obligation the modeled facts discharge (emitted with the proof carried), no unique preserving image (a residual with an addressee) -- and a refusal in any direction is a residual, never an approximation. `v2.std.coercion` already returns only a witness with a quality or a typed mismatch kind; what it lacks is the addressee and the named conversion plans an author answers with (DESIGN section 4's `bool_indicator` is the one worked example). The routes remain distinct: a coercion residual is about meaning and no provider solves it; an environmental residual is about the world and no homomorphism solves it."),
    h3(text: "1.5 The one semantic addition, and two obligations beside it"),
    p(text: "Almost all of this is already supported: sole constructors and admit_callers confine minting, D13 (`gunbc.plans.demand_engine_program` section 7) derives dependency demand from resolved references and binds providers at composition roots, the demand engine deduplicates and seals, the materialization ladder refuses uncovered recurrence, the coercion fold refuses rather than approximates. What is NOT supported today is the hole itself: the compiler floor (DESIGN section 4b) requires applications to bind in exact bijection, so an omitted operand is a refusal, not a variable. The one semantic addition is therefore ELABORATION OF OMITTED OPERANDS: the authored program may be partial, and the bijection holds on the completed program. It is D13 generalized from resource interfaces to every operand, and no syntax is added for it. Two admission obligations exceed D13 and are stated here so they are not smuggled in as widening: a derivable-operand rule over COMPLETE applications -- an operand whose type has a completion route must be omitted, or supplied with a stated divergence, so the compiler examines applications where nothing is omitted; and lifecycle execution provenance for provider-minted values, which is established by the library's sealed carriers and the domain mint's admission rather than by demand derivation, at the rung the environmental specialization states."),

    h2(text: "2. The two defining features: completion and lenses"),
    p(text: "Completion derives everything the model justifies and exposes only the residual. LENSES prove that the authored model, the derived completion, the realization, the observation and the evidence still correspond: `v2.lens.dependency_fidelity` defines correctness as declared structure matching by-execution structure, and `v2.lens.mutation_adequacy` asks whether a change to a relationship is killed. They are the reflective half of the same identity -- a pure reader over the same Node tree, storing nothing (DESIGN section 6) -- and the law for both halves is the same: a lens never repairs what it finds, exactly as completion never guesses. Stated together, v2's identity is: derive what the model determines, refuse what it does not, and prove that every later form of the program still agrees with the authored one."),
    code(text: "Completion:   derive everything the model justifies; expose only the residual.\nLenses:       prove that authored, completed, realized, observed and evidenced forms still correspond.\n"),

    h2(text: "3. The pipeline, and where lenses run"),
    code(text: "authored partial program\n   |  ingest: the grammar rows read forward (v2.compiler tokenize, parse, ingest)\n   v\ningested core structure (Node + Edge, six behaviors)\n   |  resolve and infer (v2.compiler resolve, name_resolve, normalize, infer; v2.std.inhabitance)\n   v\nresolved and inferred program\n   |\n   v\nSTATIC COMPLETION FIXED POINT -- selection and completion recurse into each other until no new hole appears:\n   1. bind logical placement and the composition root's policy\n   2. select a unique semantic realization, provider or route for each interface\n   3. derive that selection's typed prerequisites\n   4. complete those prerequisites\n   5. repeat until no new holes appear\n   |  zero route      -> residual\n   |  multiple routes -> residual\n   |  cycle, no base  -> residual\n   |  closed          -> continue\n   v\ncompleted, closed plan: every choice fixed; nothing after this point may add a node\n   |  derive, minimize and seal the demand graph (D13; v2.std.demand_engine seals here)\n   v\nsealed demand graph\n   |\n   v\nEXECUTE THE ALREADY-SELECTED PLAN:\n   materialize or reuse the selected artifacts through the selected route (v2.std.materialize, std.materialization_ladder)\n   acquire volatile leases and capacity; redeem admitted grants\n   schedule demands; run effects; observe, reconcile and mint results\n   evaluation (v2.compiler eval) or target emission (v2.compiler emit, translate, target carriers; extdeps.languages rows read backward)\n   v\nobservations, receipts and evidence\n\nlenses run across the arrows:\n   authored    <-> completed       (no completion exceeds what structure justifies)\n   declared    <-> consumed        (DESIGN section 3c: nothing dangles)\n   completed   <-> realized        (the realization preserves the modeled behavior)\n   realized    <-> observed        (readback, incarnation, freshness)\n   claim       <-> execution       (a witness runs the real route)\n   dependency  <-> interaction     (v2.lens.dependency_fidelity)\n"),
    p(text: "The decision/execution split, noun by noun, because several nouns name both a choice and its performance: BEFORE sealing -- select CLI git versus native SCM; bind logical placement and principal; select the package, image or materialization route; bind grant authority and the required admission; derive every transitive prerequisite. AFTER sealing -- execute the selected realization; allocate capacity or acquire a lease; materialize or reuse through the selected route; perform the admitted effect; observe, reconcile and mint results. So 'selected realization' below the seal means execution of the selected realization, and the materialization ROUTE is chosen before sealing while the materialization WORK happens after. This is the law the dependency design already reached: recursive provider expansion finishes before the engine seals, and a requirement discovered only at runtime is represented by a statically known resolver demand whose result is checked before consumption, never by a node added later."),
    p(text: "Where the environmental specialization sits, read off the pipeline: an ordinary call omits a `MaterializedProgram`; completion derives the environmental target; the root's blueprint closes it; the DependencyRelations are emitted; the demand engine runs the resulting closed graph. The demand engine does not own the omission or the completion law. It consumes their product."),

    h2(text: "4. Stages, their authorities today, and where the law holds"),
    p(text: "The audit behind this table was of the governing authorities and representative implementations, not of every plan document; the absence of a semantic document hierarchy made such an audit non-repeatable, which is the first finding and the reason section 6 exists. Standing is stated at the evidence level the authority itself carries."),
    table3(
      header: r3(a: "Stage", b: "Authority today", c: "Standing against the law"),
      rows: [
        r3(a: "DESIGN.md", b: "`gunbc.design_document`", c: "implicitly aligned: section 2 models once and derives every use, section 3 forbids second authorities, section 4 makes ingest, emit and coercion one procedure, section 5 refuses fallbacks; it does not yet state 'the author supplies only residuals' as one compiler law (section 7 below)"),
        r3(a: "grammar ingestion and emission", b: "the grammar rows read in both directions (DESIGN section 4); `extdeps.languages`", c: "strong pass: one grammar, forward for ingest and backward for emit"),
        r3(a: "application and type floor", b: "`v2.compiler` infer; DESIGN section 4b floor", c: "the current blocker: exact argument bijection treats an omitted operand as an error; elaboration of omitted operands is the one semantic addition (1.5)"),
        r3(a: "coercion", b: "`v2.std.coercion`, `v2.std.inhabitance`", c: "strong kernel, incomplete architecture: witness-or-typed-mismatch already, no addressee and no whole-program placement yet (1.4)"),
        r3(a: "completion, residual and closure", b: "this document, section 1", c: "stated; unbuilt; its environmental specialization is the first vertical"),
        r3(a: "demand derivation and the demand engine", b: "`gunbc.plans.demand_engine_program` (D1-D13), `v2.std.demand_engine`", c: "strong pass within scope: ordinary sequential programs induce demands and relations without feature-authored rows"),
        r3(a: "materialization", b: "`std.materialization_ladder`, `v2.std.materialize`", c: "conceptually strong, unevenly applied: derives recurrence and provider obligations; the rule that the compiler reports only why reuse cannot be decided is not yet positioned above it"),
        r3(a: "environmental completion", b: "`gunbc.plans.ensure_closure_design` (D14)", c: "strong design, not yet substrate: the developer supplies residuals, the host closure is derived, ensure is one mechanism, no syntax duplicates structure"),
        r3(a: "CLI and realization", b: "`extdeps.exec`, `gunbc.command_runner`, `gunbc.cli_dispatch_surface`", c: "mixed: raw program spellings, independently supplied packages and ambient execution routes remain (the D14 census)"),
        r3(a: "deployment layout", b: "D14 section 3.3", c: "strong proposed model: small provider-family policy at the root, large concrete closure derived per executable"),
        r3(a: "dependency relations", b: "`v2.std.dependency`", c: "partially aligned: structural, execution and provenance readings are unevenly integrated; D14's relation step owns the projection"),
        r3(a: "lenses", b: "`v2.lens.dependency_fidelity`, `v2.lens.mutation_adequacy`", c: "strong capability, weak placement: they are the reflective half (section 2) and no compiler-root design said so until this one"),
        r3(a: "diagnostics", b: "typed, located refusals throughout", c: "partial: a uniform residual with provenance, addressee and available answer routes is not yet universal"),
        r3(a: "plan document graph", b: "`gunbc.plan_registry`, now `gunbc.plan_governance`", c: "was a fail -- plans were inventoried, not placed; the relation and its lens claims land with this document (section 6)"),
      ]
    ),
    p(text: "The conclusion the table supports: the law is already present in several of v2's best designs, and it was not the compiler's named organizing law, which is why each lane kept partially rediscovering it."),

    h2(text: "5. Specializations and their boundaries"),
    p(text: "Every child of this document applies section 1 to one route. The routes stay distinct; a design that routes one through another has found the next absorbing word. The children today and the frontier beside them:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Environmental completion at a placement** -- `gunbc.plans.ensure_closure_design` (D14): targets, provider families, host blueprint and derived closure, the ensure lifecycle, the git vertical. A compiler-stage specialization, governed here."),
      li(text: "**Demand minimization and the demand engine** -- `gunbc.plans.demand_engine_program`: consumes the completed, closed program; owns demand identity, minimization, materialization and realization selection, placement. A compiler-stage authority, governed here; D14's numbering inside it is historical."),
      li(text: "**Coercion and named conversions** -- frontier: `v2.std.coercion` gains the residual's addressee and, per mismatch kind, the named conversion plans an author answers with; whole-program placement in the pipeline; first instrument, one core module emitted to the two targets that already have rows (Rust, TypeScript) with the mismatch list per target."),
      li(text: "**Materialization** -- frontier: position the rule 'the compiler reports why it cannot reuse, the developer answers' above the ladder; the one new residual carrier is tolerated staleness for a world read."),
      li(text: "**CLI construction** -- frontier: the flag table is the completed object; the user-facing surface is exactly the residual; a flag that reaches a caller while derivable is a counted defect."),
      li(text: "**Evidence** -- the lenses of section 2; cross-cutting, governed here when their plans are rowed."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "6. The plan document graph follows the same law"),
    p(text: "The document graph was repaired the way the law says, not by adding reciprocal 'see also' paragraphs to every markdown file, which would recreate the problem at the documentation layer. The author provides the one fact the repository cannot derive -- this plan is governed by that architecture node, in this role -- as one row of `gunbc.plan_governance` `plan_governance_rows`. Everything a reader sees is derived from it by `gunbc.plan` `plan_to_blocks`: the governance banner on the child, the children list on the parent. The `Plan` carrier itself is unchanged, because the relation is a fact ABOUT plans and lives beside them, keyed on the slug the generated-artifact registry already deduplicates by."),
    p(text: "The lens claims, enrolled in `test.claim.plan_governance_witness`: the compiler root is a registered plan with no governor; every governance row names two registered plans; every governed plan reaches the compiler root; and, as discriminating controls over synthetic row sets, a cycle does not reach the root and an orphan does not reach the root. Deferred, each with its trigger: a top-down index and backlinks projection (trigger: a second level of governance exists); 'every stage has exactly one authority or one explicit frontier' (trigger: stages become rows rather than this document's table); 'no child restates a universal law owned by an ancestor' (trigger: a lens over plan bodies, which today would be a grep); the DESIGN.md link (section 7)."),

    h2(text: "7. The ruling to add to DESIGN.md"),
    p(text: "DESIGN.md is a projection of `gunbc.design_document` and is never hand-edited, so this is a row for that authority in its own change, stated here so the lane that lands it has the text. It carries only the compact ruling and links to this document; the mechanism stays here."),
    quote(blocks: [
      p(text: "**Compiler completion.** An authored program may omit any fact the compiler can derive uniquely and lawfully from its modeled structure and authorities. The compiler completes those facts and exposes only what it cannot determine as typed residuals carrying provenance and an addressee. Supplying a derivable fact is a second authority unless declared as a policy departure. Libraries may expose residuals; runnable programs must close them. Lenses compare the authored, completed, realized, observed and evidenced forms and refuse unmodeled divergence. The architecture: docs/plans/v2-compiler-architecture.md."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "8. Open decisions"),
    ol(items: [
      li(text: "Whether the stage table of section 4 becomes rows (a `CompilerStage` roster with one authority or one explicit frontier each) so the 'exactly one authority per stage' lens is a fold rather than prose; this document assumes yes, with the trigger named in section 6."),
      li(text: "The spelling of the policy-departure form that lets a call site supply a derivable fact as a stated divergence; required before the derivable-operand rule rejects anything (D14 section 15)."),
      li(text: "Whether `gunbc.plan_governance` rows should also carry the compiler stage as a declaration reference rather than a label, once decision 1 lands."),
    ]),
  ]
}

data v2_compiler_architecture_plan: Plan = Plan {
  projection: PlanProjectsCommittedMarkdown,
  slug: "v2-compiler-architecture",
  title: "v2 compiler architecture: completion, residual, closure, and the lenses that prove them",
  body: v2_compiler_architecture_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen { condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Delete this document when the law of its section 1 is carried by gunbc.design_document as a ruling and by the compiler's own carriers -- elaboration of omitted operands on the floor, residuals as a typed carrier with provenance and addressee consumed by every stage's refusal path, closure refused at runnable roots -- and when the stage roster of its section 4 is rows with one authority or one explicit frontier each, enrolled under the plan-governance lens. At that point the carriers, the stage rows and their witnesses are the authority for the architecture, and this document would be a second statement of it.") }
}
